Below is a compact, self‑contained C++ program that touches almost every part of the Standard Template Library (STL).  
The comments explain what each STL container or algorithm is doing, and the code demonstrates the modern‑C++ “auto”, range‑based for‑loops, lambdas, and other idioms.

```cpp
/*********************************************************************
 *  STL Demo
 *
 *  This program shows off:
 *      • Containers: vector, list, map, set
 *      • Iterators & auto
 *      • Algorithms: sort, accumulate, find, for_each, transform
 *      • Lambdas and range‑based for‑loops
 *********************************************************************/

#include <iostream>      // std::cout, std::endl
#include <vector>        // std::vector
#include <list>          // std::list
#include <map>           // std::map
#include <set>           // std::set
#include <algorithm>     // sort, find, for_each, transform
#include <numeric>       // accumulate
#include <string>        // std::string

int main()
{
    /* ---------- 1. std::vector  ---------- */
    std::vector<int> numbers = {5, 3, 9, 1, 4};

    // Sort the vector in ascending order
    std::sort(numbers.begin(), numbers.end());

    // Sum all elements (O(n))
    int total = std::accumulate(numbers.begin(), numbers.end(), 0);
    std::cout << "Total sum: " << total << '\n';

    // Find a particular value
    auto it = std::find(numbers.begin(), numbers.end(), 4);
    if (it != numbers.end())
        std::cout << "Found 4 at index " << std::distance(numbers.begin(), it) << '\n';

    // Print each element with a lambda (C++11)
    std::cout << "Numbers: ";
    std::for_each(numbers.begin(), numbers.end(),
        [](int n){ std::cout << n << ' '; });
    std::cout << '\n';

    /* ---------- 2. std::list  ---------- */
    std::list<std::string> names = {"Alice", "Bob", "Charlie", "Alice"};
    // Remove duplicate names using a std::set as a filter
    std::set<std::string> seen;
    for (auto it = names.begin(); it != names.end(); ) {
        if (!seen.insert(*it).second)   // insert returns {iterator, bool}
            it = names.erase(it);       // erase returns next iterator
        else
            ++it;
    }

    std::cout << "Unique names (list): ";
    for (const auto& name : names) std::cout << name << ' ';
    std::cout << '\n';

    /* ---------- 3. std::map  ---------- */
    // Count how many times each word appears
    std::vector<std::string> words = {"apple", "banana", "apple", "orange", "banana"};
    std::map<std::string, int> wordCount;
    for (const auto& w : words) {
        ++wordCount[w];                // operator[] inserts default 0 if key absent
    }

    std::cout << "Word frequencies:\n";
    for (const auto& [word, cnt] : wordCount)   // C++17 structured binding
        std::cout << "  " << word << ": " << cnt << '\n';

    /* ---------- 4. std::set  ---------- */
    std::set<int> uniqueNums(numbers.begin(), numbers.end());   // constructor from iterators
    std::cout << "Unique numbers (set): ";
    for (int n : uniqueNums) std::cout << n << ' ';
    std::cout << '\n';

    /* ---------- 5. std::transform  ---------- */
    // Square each number into a new vector
    std::vector<int> squares(numbers.size());
    std::transform(numbers.begin(), numbers.end(),
                   squares.begin(),
                   [](int n){ return n * n; });

    std::cout << "Squares: ";
    for (int sq : squares) std::cout << sq << ' ';
    std::cout << '\n';

    /* ---------- 6. Range‑based for & auto ---------- */
    // Print all elements of the vector using auto
    std::cout << "Vector elements via auto: ";
    for (auto n : numbers) std::cout << n << ' ';
    std::cout << '\n';

    return 0;
}
```

### What you’ll notice

| Feature | Line(s) | Purpose |
|---------|---------|---------|
| `std::vector<int>` | 12 | Dynamic array with random‑access iterators |
| `std::sort` | 15 | Standard algorithm – O(n log n) |
| `std::accumulate` | 18 | Summation – O(n) |
| `std::find` | 22 | Linear search – O(n) |
| `std::for_each` + lambda | 27 | Functional style printing |
| `std::list<std::string>` | 34 | Bidirectional linked list |
| `std::set<std::string>` + erase | 37‑45 | Remove duplicates from a list |
| `std::map<std::string,int>` | 53 | Associative array – key/value pairs |
| `std::set<int>` | 64 | Sorted unique container |
| `std::transform` + lambda | 71 | Element‑wise transformation |
| Range‑based for & `auto` | 80‑83 | Modern C++ iteration |

Compile with any modern compiler:

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 stl_demo.cpp -O2 -Wall -Wextra -o stl_demo
./stl_demo
```

The program will produce output that demonstrates how STL containers, iterators, and algorithms work together to make generic, efficient, and expressive C++ code.
